{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-a0d02e35319af730", "type": "tool_result", "content": "200\ttutorial\n201\t\n202\tInstructional video teaching how\n203\tto play or sing a song.\n204\t\n205\tDataset\n206\t\n207\tVersions per work\n208\tSubset Works Versions Max. Mean Med.\n209\tTrain 17,319 158,485\n210\tValid 1,914 17,268\n211\tTest\n212\t3,015 32,650\n213\t\n214\t61\n215\t52\n216\t60\n217\t\n218\t9\n219\t9\n220\t11\n221\t\n222\t5\n223\t5\n224\t7\n225\t\n226\tTrain 75,514 483,187\n227\tDiVers-S Valid 8,410 52,746\n228\tTest\n229\t9,572 143,436\n230\t\n231\t443\n232\t248\n233\t623\n234\t\n235\t6\n236\t6\n237\t15\n238\t\n239\t3\n240\t3\n241\t5\n242\t\n243\tpredictions for removal of candidates containing no musical\n244\tsegments before deduplication of YVI-L. These account\n245\tfor less than 1% of the data. The resulting segment-level\n246\tpredictions are retained to support downstream analyses.\n247\t\n248\tTrain 35,278 487,815\n249\tValid 3,870 53,567\n250\tTest\n251\t5,158 88,154\n252\t\n253\t86\n254\t82\n255\t79\n256\t\n257\t14\n258\t14\n259\t17\n260\t\n261\t8\n262\t8\n263\t11\n264\t\n265\tTrain 77,019 814,189\n266\tDiVers-L Valid 8,570 89,241\n267\tTest\n268\t9,671 198,887\n269\t\n270\t461\n271\t259\n272\t633\n273\t\n274\t11\n275\t10\n276\t21\n277\t\n278\t4\n279\t4\n280\t7\n281\t\n282\t2.3 Tag Matching\n283\t\n284\tTable 2. Number of works and versions per dataset. The\n285\tminimum number of versions per work is 2 for all subsets.\n286\t\n287\tYVI-S\n288\t\n289\tTable 1. Examples of newly defined tags.\n290\tYVI-L\n291\t\n292\tTags provide categorical descriptors of musical properties, such as genres (e.g., rock, jazz), instruments\n293\t(e.g., guitar, piano), or other attributes (e.g., live,\n294\tacoustic, solo). Ideally, version representations\n295\tshould be invariant to such properties; however, existing VI\n296\tsystems have been shown to exhibit biases with respect to\n297\tthem [11]. We therefore incorporate tags as auxiliary labels\n298\tto support downstream analyses and stratified evaluation of\n299\trepresentation robustness.\n300\tWe construct a candidate tag set by merging two standard\n301\tmusic auto-tagging datasets: MTG-Jamendo (MTG) [26]\n302\tand MagnaTagATune (MTT) [27]. MTG provides 195\n303\ttags covering genres, instruments, and moods/themes, with\n304\tmulti-word tags concatenated (e.g., heavymetal); we\n305\trestore spaces to align with MTT. MTT contributes 188\n306\ttags, including genres, instruments, and other descriptors\n307\t(e.g., beat, solo, live). After removing 68 overlapping tags, the merged set comprises 315 tags. We further\n308\tadd 14 YouTube-specific tags inspired by [11] and frequent\n309\tn-grams observed in metadata (see Table 1), yielding 329\n310\ttags in total. While some of these tags extend beyond\n311\tthe traditional notion of a version (e.g., tutorial), they\n312\tcapture practically relevant variations and offer a broader\n313\tperspective on real-world content. To increase coverage, we\n314\ttranslate all tags into French, German, Italian, Portuguese,\n315\tand Spanish using GPT-5 [28], followed by manual curation to ensure translation quality. We then match tags\n316\tto YouTube video titles. 6 Matching is exact (rather than\n317\tfuzzy), as translations already provide flexibility. To focus\n318\ton contextual information (e.g., performance characteristics), matches appearing in track titles or performer names\n319\tare excluded.\n320\tWe evaluate tag quality by manually annotating 30\n321\tvideos for each of the 15 most frequent tags, resulting\n322\tin a total of 450 annotated videos. Since the annotation\n323\tconsiders only automatically assigned tags, this protocol\n324\testimates precision but not recall. Each video was independently annotated by two annotators, resulting in a Cohen’s\n325\t6 User-provided YouTube tags and tags detected in the description were\n326\tfound to be unreliable and are therefore ignored.\n327\t\n328\tκ = 0.75. Based on these annotations, the precision of automatically assigned tags exceeds 80% for nearly all evaluated\n329\ttags, with the exceptions of rock, blues, and solo. For\n330\tsolo, further inspection revealed that four videos contain\n331\tsolo sections accompanied by other instruments. While\n332\tthese are not purely solo performances, the highlighted\n333\tpassages nevertheless justify the assigned tag, illustrating\n334\tthe inherent ambiguity of this category and suggesting that\n335\tfuture work could benefit from finer distinctions.\n336\tGiven this encouraging precision, we use the automatically assigned tags as silver-label proxies for contextual\n337\tinformation in our evaluation, while acknowledging that\n338\tsome uncertainty remains due to the automatic tagging process. Specifically, we define YVI-S as the subset of YVI-L\n339\tcontaining videos whose titles include at least one tag, and\n340\tDiVers-S as the union of DVI∗ and YVI-S. These subsets\n341\tprovide smaller alternatives to the full datasets while enabling analyses across different contextual categories.\n342\t2.4 Analysis\n343\tTable 2 reports the statistics of our proposed datasets. All exhibit a long-tail distribution in versions per work, reflected\n344\tby the mean, median, and maximum statistics, consistent\n345\twith real-world characteristics and the design goals of DVI.\n346\tThe YVI datasets contain a relatively high number of versions per work, but cover a smaller number of works than\n347\tDVI. Thus, DiVers-L balances high class diversity with a\n348\tlarge number of total versions by combining strengths of\n349\tboth datasets.\n350\tFigure 2 shows the most frequently occurring tags after\n351\tmatching the original English tags and their translations.\n352\tFour of our newly defined tags appear among the most\n353\tcommon ones in video titles, namely cover, karaoke,\n354\ttutorial, and reaction, highlighting the diverse contexts in which music is reinterpreted on YouTube.\n355\tBased on our sound-event–based music/non-music predictions, we find that our dataset contains 64,349 hours of\n356\tmusic and 2,426 hours of non-music. The mean length of\n357\t\n358\t\fr\n359\tve\n360\tcoive\n361\tl ar\n362\tit\n363\tguaoke\n364\tl\n365\tr\n366\tkaorian\n367\to\n368\tt\n369\ttuactiic\n370\tt\n371\te\n372\tr ous al\n373\tacfici\n374\tof\n375\t\n376\t0\n377\t\n378\t5.16%\n379\t\n380\t1.73%\n381\t1.38%\n382\t0.86%\n383\t0.83%\n384\t0.64%\n385\t0.61%\n386\t\n387\t6.37%\n388\t\n389\t10 k 20 k 30 k 40 k 50 k 60 k 70 k 80 k\n390\tAbsolute Count\n391\t\n392\tFigure 2. Most frequent matched tags in DiVers-L.\n393\tthe longest music segment per track is 194 seconds, compared to 15 seconds for non-music segments.\n394\t3. EXPERIMENTS\n395\tWe compare systems trained or fine-tuned on DiVers against\n396\testablished baselines along three evaluation axes. First, we\n397\tassess overall retrieval accuracy across three data regimes:\n398\tCataloged (based on SHS and Discogs), In-the-Wild (unconstrained web-sourced audio), and their combination (Section 3.3.1). Second, we conduct stratified experiments to\n399\tidentify where gains are most pronounced, examining performance across tag-defined version types and non-music\n400\tratios (Section 3.3.2). Third, we analyze how fine-tuning\n401\treshapes embedding space geometry across DVI∗ and YVI\n402\t(Section 3.3.3).\n403\t3.1 Training on DiVers-S and DiVers-L\n404\tWe evaluate the effectiveness of the proposed datasets for\n405\tboth training from scratch (Section 3.1.1) and fine-tuning\n406\t(Section 3.1.2) a state-of-the-art system.\n407\tFor each version, we extract a randomly selected 2.5minute segment from the original audio. We compute the\n408\tconstant-Q transform (CQT) using a hop size of 20 milliseconds, spanning 7 octaves starting at C1 with 12 bins per\n409\toctave. The resulting representation is downsampled along\n410\tthe time axis by a factor of 5 and normalized to the range\n411\t[0, 1]. Following [29], we apply SpecAugment [30], as well\n412\tas pitch-roll and time-stretch augmentations. Model performance is evaluated using the same combined validation\n413\tmetric as [29], namely the geometric mean of mean average\n414\tprecision (MAP) and normalized average rank (NAR).\n415\t3.1.1 Training DVINetX\n416\tWe adopt the architecture of DVINet+ [4] and increase\n417\tthe model capacity to 20.7 million parameters by scaling\n418\tthe number of channels from 48 to 64 and the embedding\n419\tdimension from 512 to 1,024. We refer to the resulting\n420\tmodel as DVINetX, which we train using triplet loss with\n421\thard triplet mining and a margin of m = 0.3. Optimization\n422\tis performed using Adam with an initial learning rate of 3 ×\n423\t10−4 and a reduce-on-plateau scheduler with a patience of\n424\t10 epochs. One epoch is defined as all versions being used\n425\tas anchors. Each batch contains 24 works, with 5 versions\n426\tper work. For works with fewer than 5 versions, samples\n427\tare duplicated at random to meet this requirement. With\n428\tthese configurations, training requires 5 days on DiVers-S\n429\tand 7.5 days on DiVers-L on a single NVIDIA H100 NVL\n430\tGPU.\n431\t\n432\t3.1.2 Fine-tuning CLEWS\n433\tWe fine-tune pre-trained CLEWS on DiVers-S using the\n434\tbest-pair-without-replacement strategy (bpwr-5) with k =\n435\t5, following the original proposed setup [6]. The method\n436\toperates on non-overlapping 20-second segments extracted\n437\tfrom the 2.5-minute track excerpts and selects the best segment pairs across pairs of versions. Training is conducted\n438\tfor up to 40 epochs using AdamW with a cosine learningrate schedule, an initial learning rate of 5×10−5 , and weight\n439\tdecay of 10−3 . An epoch is defined such that all versions\n440\tfrom DVI∗ are used as anchors. Due to GPU constraints,\n441\twe construct batches of 25 works, each represented by 4 versions. As before, works with fewer available versions are\n442\toversampled to meet this requirement. Fine-tuning takes 4\n443\tdays on two NVIDIA H100 NVL GPUs.\n444\t3.2 Evaluation\n445\tWe evaluate our methods on our proposed test sets listed\n446\tin Table 2, as well as the test subset of DVI∗ . Furthermore,\n447\twe evaluate on the established SHS100K dataset [12] representing another dataset in the domain of cataloged versions.\n448\tWe retrieve approximately 81% of the referenced versions\n449\tfrom YouTube, which is comparable to prior reported coverage [6, 7]; we denote this subset as SHS100K∗ . We further\n450\tinclude SHS-YT [11], restricted to works with a minimum\n451\tof two versions, forming a smaller dataset of in-the-wild\n452\tversions, which we denote as SHS-YT+ . Both additional\n453\tdatasets share works exclusively with the test split of DVI,\n454\tensuring no data leakage.\n455\tWe adopt the full-track evaluation protocol, representing each track with a single embedding as in common VI\n456\tbenchmarks [7, 12, 31]. In our stratified experiment, we\n457\tcompare this global retrieval strategy against the segmentlevel matching approach of [6], which extracts 20-second\n458\tsegments with a 5-second hop, computes distances between\n459\tsegments, and reduces them into track-level distances using the bpwr-10 strategy. For both retrieval strategies, we\n460\trestrict each version to its first 10 minutes to reduce computational cost. We further evaluate the effect of applying L2 -normalization prior to distance computation for the\n461\tCLEWS variants. Notably, DVINet+ and DVINetX already\n462\tincorporate this normalization during training. In all experiments, we report MAP and NAR following [6], where NAR\n463\tserves as a complementary metric to MAP by capturing\n464\toverall ranking quality beyond the top-ranked results.\n465\tIn our stratified experiments, we construct controlled\n466\tpairwise evaluation sets to simulate different retrieval conditions. Each subset contains c = 500 works, from which\n467\twe sample one query version and one candidate version\n468\tper work under the respective stratification constraints. In\n469\tthe first experiment, query versions are drawn from either\n470\tDVI∗ or YVI, while candidate versions are sampled from\n471\tsubsets defined by specific tags, enabling analysis of performance across content-related strata. In the second experiment, queries are drawn from DVI∗ and candidates are\n472\tsampled from disjoint bins defined by estimated non-music\n473\tratios. To compute these bins, we estimate the ratio of nonmusical content per version by averaging binary music/non-\n474\t\n475\t\fFull\n476\t\n477\tMAP\n478\t\n479\tSegment\n480\t\n481\tmusic predictions over time, obtained from PANN using\n482\t10-second segments with a 1-second hop (see Section 2.2),\n483\tand partition the resulting ratios into non-overlapping intervals of width 0.2. This setup yields balanced and controlled\n484\tpairwise comparisons across conditions, enabling analysis\n485\tof retrieval behavior under systematic variations in version\n486\ttype and non-music ratio.\n487\t\n488\tL2 0.76 0.68 0.47 0.18\n489\t\n490\tNAR\n491\t\n492\tBetter\n493\t4.93 7.06 18.34 35.40\n494\t\n495\tFT+L2 0.81 0.73 0.52 0.23\n496\t\n497\t3.64 5.29 14.56 28.64\n498\t\n499\tL2 0.79 0.72 0.52 0.22\n500\t\n501\t6.30 9.35 20.06 36.03\n502\t\n503\tFT+L2 0.83 0.75 0.57 0.26\n504\t0.2 0.4 0.6 0.8\n505\tNon-music ratio\n506\t\n507\t4.80 7.77 16.47 30.57\n508\t0.2 0.4 0.6 0.8 Worse\n509\t\n510\tNon-music ratio\n511\t\n512\t3.3 Results\n513\t3.3.1 Overall Retrieval Accuracy\n514\tWe report the overall performance of our trained VI systems\n515\tand established baselines trained on DVI in Table 3. We\n516\tobserve that L2 normalization of the embeddings at inference consistently improves performance for both CLEWS\n517\tvariants. CLEWSFT+L2 achieves the best performance on\n518\tthe YVI datasets, SHS100K∗ and on DiVers-L. It performs\n519\ton par with CLEWSL2 on DiVers-S and DVI∗ in terms of\n520\tNAR, but underperforms compared to CLEWSL2 in the\n521\tMAP setting on DVI∗ . This points to a trade-off introduced\n522\tby fine-tuning, improving robustness in noisy conditions\n523\twhile yielding more limited gains on cleaner datasets such\n524\tas DVI∗ . The contrasting behavior on DVI∗ and SHS100K∗\n525\tmay be partly explained by differences in class size: their\n526\tmedian class sizes are 3 and 12 versions per work, respectively. Consequently, MAP is more sensitive to individual\n527\tretrieval failures on DVI∗ .\n528\tComparing the DVINetX variants with respect to their\n529\ttraining data, we observe that training on DVI∗ yields the\n530\tbest performance when evaluated on its own test subset. On\n531\tall other evaluation datasets, training on DiVers-S improves\n532\tNAR over training on DVI∗ , whereas the corresponding\n533\tMAP gains are confined to the in-the-wild subsets YVI-S\n534\tand YVI-L; on SHS100K∗ and the combined subsets, MAP\n535\tremains slightly below the DVI∗ -trained model. Scaling to\n536\tDiVers-L improves over DiVers-S on both metrics and on\n537\tevery evaluation dataset, and additionally surpasses training\n538\ton DVI∗ everywhere except on the DVI∗ test subset itself.\n539\tThis demonstrates a clear benefit of scaling the training\n540\tdata.\n541\tWe further compare CLEWSL2 and CLEWSFT+L2 , as\n542\twell as the DVINetX variants on SHS-YT+ . On this dataset,\n543\tCLEWSL2 obtains a MAP of 0.686 and an NAR of 10.22,\n544\twhile CLEWSFT+L2 improves MAP to 0.708 at the cost of\n545\ta slightly worse normalized average rank of 10.59. For\n546\tDVINetX, its variants trained on DiVers-S and DiVers-L\n547\tachieve MAP scores of 0.636 and 0.665 respectively. Measured in NAR, the system even outperforms the CLEWS\n548\tvariants with values of 9.87 and 9.61. This is notable, because the system has only 20 million parameters compared\n549\tto nearly 200 million in CLEWS.\n550\t3.3.2 Performance Across Data Strata\n551\tIn Table 4, we report the difference ∆ between\n552\tCLEWSFT+L2 and CLEWSL2 across stratified test subsets.\n553\tGains are observed across almost all tag-based candidate\n554\tstrata and both query domains, with the strongest improvements when using YVI versions as queries. The largest and\n555\tmost consistent gains are obtained for the tutorial tag,\n556\t\n557\tFigure 3. Retrieval performance of CLEWS variants compared across full track retrieval and segment-level matching\n558\twith subsequent bpwr-10 reduction as proposed by [6].\n559\t\n560\twhich we attribute to its association with higher noise levels: tutorial content likely contains spoken segments,\n561\trepeated sections, and variable pacing. In contrast, live\n562\tshows smaller and less consistent improvements, suggesting that fine-tuning is less effective when candidate noise\n563\tlevels are lower.\n564\tWe next examine whether these gains persist across candidate non-music ratios and retrieval strategies (Figure 3).\n565\tThe evaluated strategies range from efficient full-track embedding comparison to more fine-grained segment-level\n566\tmatching. Across both metrics, both strategies, and all\n567\tnon-music ratios, CLEWSFT+L2 consistently outperforms\n568\tCLEWSL2 . The margin is most evident for NAR, where the\n569\tgap widens with the non-music ratio, while the MAP gains\n570\tstay in a narrow band of 0.03 to 0.05 absolute. Comparing\n571\tthe two retrieval strategies, segment-level matching attains\n572\tthe higher MAP, but is consistently worse in NAR, suggesting that local matching sharpens the top of the ranking while\n573\tbeing more susceptible to spurious segment-level matches\n574\tfurther down. Notably, full-track CLEWSFT+L2 matches or\n575\tsurpasses segment-level CLEWSL2 on both metrics at every ratio, despite being substantially cheaper at query time.\n576\tOverall, these results suggest that the improved robustness\n577\tof CLEWSFT+L2 is also driven by better aggregation along\n578\tthe temporal axis, allowing the model to handle non-music\n579\tand local distortions within a track.\n580\tComplementing the results shown in Figure 3, we further\n581\tanalyze the effect of L2 normalization across retrieval strategies and non-music ratios. The dominant trend is that it\n582\tprovides substantial gains for full-track embeddings, while\n583\toffering little benefit – and occasionally slight degradation\n584\t– for segment-level matching. For full-track embeddings,\n585\tMAP improves by 0.024 – 0.048 and NAR decreases by up\n586\tto 3.13, with gains generally increasing with non-music and\n587\tbeing most pronounced for the fine-tuned model. In contrast, for segment-level matching, MAP gains are smaller\n588\t(up to 0.016) and NAR changes remain close to zero, with\n589\tdegradations for the base model at higher non-music ratios (0.022 and 0.143 at non-music ratios 0.6 and 0.8, respectively). Overall, this suggests that L2 normalization\n590\timproves robustness for full-track retrieval, while offering\n591\tlimited or occasionally negative effects for segment-level\n592\tmatching.\n593\t\n594\t\fCataloged\n595\tModel\n596\t\n597\tTrain\n598\t\n599\tDVI\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "3c09a112-7bf1-4bbb-ad44-b5fe73cc4ddb", "uuid": "772bfb79-6ea2-4e13-aeb7-a9a41cdda277", "timestamp": "2026-08-06T07:11:45.659Z", "tool_use_result": {"type": "text", "file": {"filePath": "/tmp/paper_2608.04543v1.txt", "content": "tutorial\n\nInstructional video teaching how\nto play or sing a song.\n\nDataset\n\nVersions per work\nSubset Works Versions Max. Mean Med.\nTrain 17,319 158,485\nValid 1,914 17,268\nTest\n3,015 32,650\n\n61\n52\n60\n\n9\n9\n11\n\n5\n5\n7\n\nTrain 75,514 483,187\nDiVers-S Valid 8,410 52,746\nTest\n9,572 143,436\n\n443\n248\n623\n\n6\n6\n15\n\n3\n3\n5\n\npredictions for removal of candidates containing no musical\nsegments before deduplication of YVI-L. These account\nfor less than 1% of the data. The resulting segment-level\npredictions are retained to support downstream analyses.\n\nTrain 35,278 487,815\nValid 3,870 53,567\nTest\n5,158 88,154\n\n86\n82\n79\n\n14\n14\n17\n\n8\n8\n11\n\nTrain 77,019 814,189\nDiVers-L Valid 8,570 89,241\nTest\n9,671 198,887\n\n461\n259\n633\n\n11\n10\n21\n\n4\n4\n7\n\n2.3 Tag Matching\n\nTable 2. Number of works and versions per dataset. The\nminimum number of versions per work is 2 for all subsets.\n\nYVI-S\n\nTable 1. Examples of newly defined tags.\nYVI-L\n\nTags provide categorical descriptors of musical properties, such as genres (e.g., rock, jazz), instruments\n(e.g., guitar, piano), or other attributes (e.g., live,\nacoustic, solo). Ideally, version representations\nshould be invariant to such properties; however, existing VI\nsystems have been shown to exhibit biases with respect to\nthem [11]. We therefore incorporate tags as auxiliary labels\nto support downstream analyses and stratified evaluation of\nrepresentation robustness.\nWe construct a candidate tag set by merging two standard\nmusic auto-tagging datasets: MTG-Jamendo (MTG) [26]\nand MagnaTagATune (MTT) [27]. MTG provides 195\ntags covering genres, instruments, and moods/themes, with\nmulti-word tags concatenated (e.g., heavymetal); we\nrestore spaces to align with MTT. MTT contributes 188\ntags, including genres, instruments, and other descriptors\n(e.g., beat, solo, live). After removing 68 overlapping tags, the merged set comprises 315 tags. We further\nadd 14 YouTube-specific tags inspired by [11] and frequent\nn-grams observed in metadata (see Table 1), yielding 329\ntags in total. While some of these tags extend beyond\nthe traditional notion of a version (e.g., tutorial), they\ncapture practically relevant variations and offer a broader\nperspective on real-world content. To increase coverage, we\ntranslate all tags into French, German, Italian, Portuguese,\nand Spanish using GPT-5 [28], followed by manual curation to ensure translation quality. We then match tags\nto YouTube video titles. 6 Matching is exact (rather than\nfuzzy), as translations already provide flexibility. To focus\non contextual information (e.g., performance characteristics), matches appearing in track titles or performer names\nare excluded.\nWe evaluate tag quality by manually annotating 30\nvideos for each of the 15 most frequent tags, resulting\nin a total of 450 annotated videos. Since the annotation\nconsiders only automatically assigned tags, this protocol\nestimates precision but not recall. Each video was independently annotated by two annotators, resulting in a Cohen’s\n6 User-provided YouTube tags and tags detected in the description were\nfound to be unreliable and are therefore ignored.\n\nκ = 0.75. Based on these annotations, the precision of automatically assigned tags exceeds 80% for nearly all evaluated\ntags, with the exceptions of rock, blues, and solo. For\nsolo, further inspection revealed that four videos contain\nsolo sections accompanied by other instruments. While\nthese are not purely solo performances, the highlighted\npassages nevertheless justify the assigned tag, illustrating\nthe inherent ambiguity of this category and suggesting that\nfuture work could benefit from finer distinctions.\nGiven this encouraging precision, we use the automatically assigned tags as silver-label proxies for contextual\ninformation in our evaluation, while acknowledging that\nsome uncertainty remains due to the automatic tagging process. Specifically, we define YVI-S as the subset of YVI-L\ncontaining videos whose titles include at least one tag, and\nDiVers-S as the union of DVI∗ and YVI-S. These subsets\nprovide smaller alternatives to the full datasets while enabling analyses across different contextual categories.\n2.4 Analysis\nTable 2 reports the statistics of our proposed datasets. All exhibit a long-tail distribution in versions per work, reflected\nby the mean, median, and maximum statistics, consistent\nwith real-world characteristics and the design goals of DVI.\nThe YVI datasets contain a relatively high number of versions per work, but cover a smaller number of works than\nDVI. Thus, DiVers-L balances high class diversity with a\nlarge number of total versions by combining strengths of\nboth datasets.\nFigure 2 shows the most frequently occurring tags after\nmatching the original English tags and their translations.\nFour of our newly defined tags appear among the most\ncommon ones in video titles, namely cover, karaoke,\ntutorial, and reaction, highlighting the diverse contexts in which music is reinterpreted on YouTube.\nBased on our sound-event–based music/non-music predictions, we find that our dataset contains 64,349 hours of\nmusic and 2,426 hours of non-music. The mean length of\n\n\fr\nve\ncoive\nl ar\nit\nguaoke\nl\nr\nkaorian\no\nt\ntuactiic\nt\ne\nr ous al\nacfici\nof\n\n0\n\n5.16%\n\n1.73%\n1.38%\n0.86%\n0.83%\n0.64%\n0.61%\n\n6.37%\n\n10 k 20 k 30 k 40 k 50 k 60 k 70 k 80 k\nAbsolute Count\n\nFigure 2. Most frequent matched tags in DiVers-L.\nthe longest music segment per track is 194 seconds, compared to 15 seconds for non-music segments.\n3. EXPERIMENTS\nWe compare systems trained or fine-tuned on DiVers against\nestablished baselines along three evaluation axes. First, we\nassess overall retrieval accuracy across three data regimes:\nCataloged (based on SHS and Discogs), In-the-Wild (unconstrained web-sourced audio), and their combination (Section 3.3.1). Second, we conduct stratified experiments to\nidentify where gains are most pronounced, examining performance across tag-defined version types and non-music\nratios (Section 3.3.2). Third, we analyze how fine-tuning\nreshapes embedding space geometry across DVI∗ and YVI\n(Section 3.3.3).\n3.1 Training on DiVers-S and DiVers-L\nWe evaluate the effectiveness of the proposed datasets for\nboth training from scratch (Section 3.1.1) and fine-tuning\n(Section 3.1.2) a state-of-the-art system.\nFor each version, we extract a randomly selected 2.5minute segment from the original audio. We compute the\nconstant-Q transform (CQT) using a hop size of 20 milliseconds, spanning 7 octaves starting at C1 with 12 bins per\noctave. The resulting representation is downsampled along\nthe time axis by a factor of 5 and normalized to the range\n[0, 1]. Following [29], we apply SpecAugment [30], as well\nas pitch-roll and time-stretch augmentations. Model performance is evaluated using the same combined validation\nmetric as [29], namely the geometric mean of mean average\nprecision (MAP) and normalized average rank (NAR).\n3.1.1 Training DVINetX\nWe adopt the architecture of DVINet+ [4] and increase\nthe model capacity to 20.7 million parameters by scaling\nthe number of channels from 48 to 64 and the embedding\ndimension from 512 to 1,024. We refer to the resulting\nmodel as DVINetX, which we train using triplet loss with\nhard triplet mining and a margin of m = 0.3. Optimization\nis performed using Adam with an initial learning rate of 3 ×\n10−4 and a reduce-on-plateau scheduler with a patience of\n10 epochs. One epoch is defined as all versions being used\nas anchors. Each batch contains 24 works, with 5 versions\nper work. For works with fewer than 5 versions, samples\nare duplicated at random to meet this requirement. With\nthese configurations, training requires 5 days on DiVers-S\nand 7.5 days on DiVers-L on a single NVIDIA H100 NVL\nGPU.\n\n3.1.2 Fine-tuning CLEWS\nWe fine-tune pre-trained CLEWS on DiVers-S using the\nbest-pair-without-replacement strategy (bpwr-5) with k =\n5, following the original proposed setup [6]. The method\noperates on non-overlapping 20-second segments extracted\nfrom the 2.5-minute track excerpts and selects the best segment pairs across pairs of versions. Training is conducted\nfor up to 40 epochs using AdamW with a cosine learningrate schedule, an initial learning rate of 5×10−5 , and weight\ndecay of 10−3 . An epoch is defined such that all versions\nfrom DVI∗ are used as anchors. Due to GPU constraints,\nwe construct batches of 25 works, each represented by 4 versions. As before, works with fewer available versions are\noversampled to meet this requirement. Fine-tuning takes 4\ndays on two NVIDIA H100 NVL GPUs.\n3.2 Evaluation\nWe evaluate our methods on our proposed test sets listed\nin Table 2, as well as the test subset of DVI∗ . Furthermore,\nwe evaluate on the established SHS100K dataset [12] representing another dataset in the domain of cataloged versions.\nWe retrieve approximately 81% of the referenced versions\nfrom YouTube, which is comparable to prior reported coverage [6, 7]; we denote this subset as SHS100K∗ . We further\ninclude SHS-YT [11], restricted to works with a minimum\nof two versions, forming a smaller dataset of in-the-wild\nversions, which we denote as SHS-YT+ . Both additional\ndatasets share works exclusively with the test split of DVI,\nensuring no data leakage.\nWe adopt the full-track evaluation protocol, representing each track with a single embedding as in common VI\nbenchmarks [7, 12, 31]. In our stratified experiment, we\ncompare this global retrieval strategy against the segmentlevel matching approach of [6], which extracts 20-second\nsegments with a 5-second hop, computes distances between\nsegments, and reduces them into track-level distances using the bpwr-10 strategy. For both retrieval strategies, we\nrestrict each version to its first 10 minutes to reduce computational cost. We further evaluate the effect of applying L2 -normalization prior to distance computation for the\nCLEWS variants. Notably, DVINet+ and DVINetX already\nincorporate this normalization during training. In all experiments, we report MAP and NAR following [6], where NAR\nserves as a complementary metric to MAP by capturing\noverall ranking quality beyond the top-ranked results.\nIn our stratified experiments, we construct controlled\npairwise evaluation sets to simulate different retrieval conditions. Each subset contains c = 500 works, from which\nwe sample one query version and one candidate version\nper work under the respective stratification constraints. In\nthe first experiment, query versions are drawn from either\nDVI∗ or YVI, while candidate versions are sampled from\nsubsets defined by specific tags, enabling analysis of performance across content-related strata. In the second experiment, queries are drawn from DVI∗ and candidates are\nsampled from disjoint bins defined by estimated non-music\nratios. To compute these bins, we estimate the ratio of nonmusical content per version by averaging binary music/non-\n\n\fFull\n\nMAP\n\nSegment\n\nmusic predictions over time, obtained from PANN using\n10-second segments with a 1-second hop (see Section 2.2),\nand partition the resulting ratios into non-overlapping intervals of width 0.2. This setup yields balanced and controlled\npairwise comparisons across conditions, enabling analysis\nof retrieval behavior under systematic variations in version\ntype and non-music ratio.\n\nL2 0.76 0.68 0.47 0.18\n\nNAR\n\nBetter\n4.93 7.06 18.34 35.40\n\nFT+L2 0.81 0.73 0.52 0.23\n\n3.64 5.29 14.56 28.64\n\nL2 0.79 0.72 0.52 0.22\n\n6.30 9.35 20.06 36.03\n\nFT+L2 0.83 0.75 0.57 0.26\n0.2 0.4 0.6 0.8\nNon-music ratio\n\n4.80 7.77 16.47 30.57\n0.2 0.4 0.6 0.8 Worse\n\nNon-music ratio\n\n3.3 Results\n3.3.1 Overall Retrieval Accuracy\nWe report the overall performance of our trained VI systems\nand established baselines trained on DVI in Table 3. We\nobserve that L2 normalization of the embeddings at inference consistently improves performance for both CLEWS\nvariants. CLEWSFT+L2 achieves the best performance on\nthe YVI datasets, SHS100K∗ and on DiVers-L. It performs\non par with CLEWSL2 on DiVers-S and DVI∗ in terms of\nNAR, but underperforms compared to CLEWSL2 in the\nMAP setting on DVI∗ . This points to a trade-off introduced\nby fine-tuning, improving robustness in noisy conditions\nwhile yielding more limited gains on cleaner datasets such\nas DVI∗ . The contrasting behavior on DVI∗ and SHS100K∗\nmay be partly explained by differences in class size: their\nmedian class sizes are 3 and 12 versions per work, respectively. Consequently, MAP is more sensitive to individual\nretrieval failures on DVI∗ .\nComparing the DVINetX variants with respect to their\ntraining data, we observe that training on DVI∗ yields the\nbest performance when evaluated on its own test subset. On\nall other evaluation datasets, training on DiVers-S improves\nNAR over training on DVI∗ , whereas the corresponding\nMAP gains are confined to the in-the-wild subsets YVI-S\nand YVI-L; on SHS100K∗ and the combined subsets, MAP\nremains slightly below the DVI∗ -trained model. Scaling to\nDiVers-L improves over DiVers-S on both metrics and on\nevery evaluation dataset, and additionally surpasses training\non DVI∗ everywhere except on the DVI∗ test subset itself.\nThis demonstrates a clear benefit of scaling the training\ndata.\nWe further compare CLEWSL2 and CLEWSFT+L2 , as\nwell as the DVINetX variants on SHS-YT+ . On this dataset,\nCLEWSL2 obtains a MAP of 0.686 and an NAR of 10.22,\nwhile CLEWSFT+L2 improves MAP to 0.708 at the cost of\na slightly worse normalized average rank of 10.59. For\nDVINetX, its variants trained on DiVers-S and DiVers-L\nachieve MAP scores of 0.636 and 0.665 respectively. Measured in NAR, the system even outperforms the CLEWS\nvariants with values of 9.87 and 9.61. This is notable, because the system has only 20 million parameters compared\nto nearly 200 million in CLEWS.\n3.3.2 Performance Across Data Strata\nIn Table 4, we report the difference ∆ between\nCLEWSFT+L2 and CLEWSL2 across stratified test subsets.\nGains are observed across almost all tag-based candidate\nstrata and both query domains, with the strongest improvements when using YVI versions as queries. The largest and\nmost consistent gains are obtained for the tutorial tag,\n\nFigure 3. Retrieval performance of CLEWS variants compared across full track retrieval and segment-level matching\nwith subsequent bpwr-10 reduction as proposed by [6].\n\nwhich we attribute to its association with higher noise levels: tutorial content likely contains spoken segments,\nrepeated sections, and variable pacing. In contrast, live\nshows smaller and less consistent improvements, suggesting that fine-tuning is less effective when candidate noise\nlevels are lower.\nWe next examine whether these gains persist across candidate non-music ratios and retrieval strategies (Figure 3).\nThe evaluated strategies range from efficient full-track embedding comparison to more fine-grained segment-level\nmatching. Across both metrics, both strategies, and all\nnon-music ratios, CLEWSFT+L2 consistently outperforms\nCLEWSL2 . The margin is most evident for NAR, where the\ngap widens with the non-music ratio, while the MAP gains\nstay in a narrow band of 0.03 to 0.05 absolute. Comparing\nthe two retrieval strategies, segment-level matching attains\nthe higher MAP, but is consistently worse in NAR, suggesting that local matching sharpens the top of the ranking while\nbeing more susceptible to spurious segment-level matches\nfurther down. Notably, full-track CLEWSFT+L2 matches or\nsurpasses segment-level CLEWSL2 on both metrics at every ratio, despite being substantially cheaper at query time.\nOverall, these results suggest that the improved robustness\nof CLEWSFT+L2 is also driven by better aggregation along\nthe temporal axis, allowing the model to handle non-music\nand local distortions within a track.\nComplementing the results shown in Figure 3, we further\nanalyze the effect of L2 normalization across retrieval strategies and non-music ratios. The dominant trend is that it\nprovides substantial gains for full-track embeddings, while\noffering little benefit – and occasionally slight degradation\n– for segment-level matching. For full-track embeddings,\nMAP improves by 0.024 – 0.048 and NAR decreases by up\nto 3.13, with gains generally increasing with non-music and\nbeing most pronounced for the fine-tuned model. In contrast, for segment-level matching, MAP gains are smaller\n(up to 0.016) and NAR changes remain close to zero, with\ndegradations for the base model at higher non-music ratios (0.022 and 0.143 at non-music ratios 0.6 and 0.8, respectively). Overall, this suggests that L2 normalization\nimproves robustness for full-track retrieval, while offering\nlimited or occasionally negative effects for segment-level\nmatching.\n\n\fCataloged\nModel\n\nTrain\n\nDVI", "numLines": 400, "startLine": 200, "totalLines": 1071}}}
