\t1.45\n768\t\n769\t.756\n770\t.771\n771\t.788\n772\t\n773\t2.47\n774\t1.95\n775\t1.68\n776\t\n777\t.674\n778\t.665\n779\t.676\n780\t\n781\t3.37\n782\t3.34\n783\t3.23\n784\t\n785\t.693\n786\t.687\n787\t.701\n788\t\n789\t3.13\n790\t2.98\n791\t2.82\n792\t\n793\tTable 3. Results of VI systems by training dataset across test subsets. Di abbreviates DiVers and CLEWSFT denotes\n794\tfine-tuned CLEWS. L2 denotes L2 -normalized embeddings at inference time. Checkpoints for trained systems on DVI are\n795\tprovided by the authors of CLEWS [6]. Bold and underlined values indicate the best and second-best results, respectively.\n796\t* indicates a statistically significant improvement of CLEWSFT+L2 over CLEWSL2 (Wilcoxon signed-rank test, HolmBonferroni-corrected p < 0.05).\n797\t\n798\tQuery Candidate\n799\t\n800\t∆ MAP ↑ ∆ NAR ↓\n801\t\n802\tDVI∗\n803\t\n804\tcover & guitar\n805\tkaraoke\n806\tlive\n807\tofficial\n808\ttutorial\n809\ttutorial & guitar\n810\t\n811\t.022*\n812\t.024*\n813\t.018*\n814\t-.002\n815\t.056*\n816\t.080*\n817\t\n818\t-0.20*\n819\t-0.08*\n820\t-0.20*\n821\t-0.42\n822\t-1.14*\n823\t-0.67*\n824\t\n825\tYVI\n826\t\n827\tcover & guitar\n828\tkaraoke\n829\tlive\n830\tofficial\n831\ttutorial\n832\ttutorial & guitar\n833\t\n834\t.028*\n835\t.027*\n836\t.013*\n837\t.005*\n838\t.080*\n839\t.080*\n840\t\n841\t-0.12*\n842\t-0.20*\n843\t-0.20\n844\t-0.36*\n845\t-0.96*\n846\t-0.71*\n847\t\n848\tTable 4. ∆ between CLEWSFT+L2 and CLEWSL2 on stratified subsets across different combinations of query domains\n849\tand candidates stratified by a respective tag. * indicates\n850\tstatistical significance (Wilcoxon signed-rank test, HolmBonferroni-corrected p < 0.05).\n851\t\n852\t3.3.3 Embedding Space Analysis\n853\tWe analyze how fine-tuning affects the embedding space\n854\tof the CLEWS variants on the DVI∗ and YVI test subsets using full-track embeddings computed over 50,000\n855\trandomly sampled test versions per dataset. We measure\n856\tintra-class distance as the mean pairwise distance between\n857\tembeddings within the same class, and inter-class distance\n858\tanalogously across classes; their ratio (inter/intra) quantifies\n859\tclass separability. Since our best-performing configurations\n860\tuse L2 -normalized embeddings, we focus on that setting\n861\tfirst.\n862\tFine-tuning increases the ratio on YVI from 3.87 to 4.00,\n863\tbut decreases it on DVI∗ from 3.43 to 3.21, mirroring the\n864\t\n865\tMAP degradation reported in Section 3.3.1. Separability on\n866\tin-the-wild versions thus improves at the cost of separability\n867\ton cleaner, cataloged ones. The latter is especially consequential in DVI∗ , where a median class size of 3 makes\n868\tMAP sensitive to the rank of individual positives.\n869\tWithout normalization, fine-tuning shrinks both distances, but the intra-class distance shrinks more, so the\n870\tratio still rises on both datasets (e.g., 2.78 → 3.23 on\n871\tDVI∗ ). Normalization then raises the ratio considerably for\n872\tthe baseline (2.78 → 3.43 on DVI∗ ) but only marginally\n873\tfor the fine-tuned model (3.23 → 3.21). A possible explanation is that embedding norms become more uniform\n874\tacross tracks after fine-tuning, with their standard deviation\n875\troughly halving on both datasets at a nearly constant mean.\n876\tNormalization may therefore alter the mean-distance geometry less after fine-tuning, although it still yields consistent\n877\tretrieval gains (Section 3.3.2).\n878\t\n879\t4. CONCLUSION AND FUTURE WORK\n880\tWe introduce DiVers, a large VI dataset incorporating unofficial and user-generated YouTube recordings, resulting\n881\tin a more acoustically heterogeneous benchmark than existing curated collections. Our experiments suggest that\n882\ttraining and fine-tuning on DiVers can improve robustness\n883\tto noisy and diverse inputs. We further observe that finetuning improves class separability on in-the-wild versions\n884\twhile reducing it on cleaner ones from DVI∗ , mirroring the\n885\tcorresponding MAP results.\n886\tFuture work should explore methods that better enforce\n887\tinter-class separation and improve the robustness of learned\n888\trepresentations. Additionally, integrating multi-view signals beyond audio embeddings – such as lyrics [8, 33] –\n889\toffers a promising direction for more robust VI under realworld conditions.\n890\t\n891\t\f5. ACKNOWLEDGMENTS\n892\t\n893\t8. REFERENCES\n894\t\n895\tWe thank our student assistants Sharleen Frankenstein, Siri\n896\tGrenzebach, Jannis Köster and Anna Schwerdtel at the\n897\tBerlin School of Library and Information Science for their\n898\tsupport in annotating the data to evaluate the precision of\n899\tthe automatically matched tags.\n900\tR. Oguz Araz is partially supported by the pre-doctoral\n901\tgrant AGAUR-FI Joan Oró (2024 FI-3 00065) and the Cátedra IA y Música project (TSI-100929-2023-1), funded by\n902\tthe Secretaría de Estado de Digitalización e Inteligencia\n903\tArtificial and European Union-Next Generation EU.\n904\t\n905\t[1] M. Abrassart and G. Doras, “And what if two musical versions don’t share melody, harmony, rhythm, or\n906\tlyrics?” in Proc. of the 23rd Int. Society for Music\n907\tInformation Retrieval Conf., 2022, pp. 677–684.\n908\t\n909\t6. AI USAGE STATEMENT\n910\tWe used large language models (LLMs) in a limited and\n911\tsupportive role during this work. Specifically, LLMs were\n912\temployed for writing style refinement and language editing,\n913\tas well as for assisting in code generation and subsequent\n914\trefinement. In addition, LLMs were used to translate the tag\n915\tvocabulary into multiple European languages to improve tag\n916\tmatching coverage. These uses did not constitute a methodological contribution to the research itself. All generated\n917\tcontent, including code and translations, was manually reviewed, validated, and, where necessary, corrected by the\n918\tauthors to ensure accuracy and consistency. The authors\n919\ttake full responsibility for all aspects of the work, including\n920\tthe correctness of the dataset, experiments, and reported\n921\tresults.\n922\t\n923\t7. ETHICS STATEMENT\n924\tThis work introduces DiVers, a collection of large-scale\n925\tdatasets of music versions collected from YouTube. All\n926\taudio content referenced in the dataset is sourced from\n927\tpublicly accessible YouTube uploads, and no copyrighted\n928\taudio is redistributed. The release contains only metadata, annotations, and derived feature representations intended for non-commercial research in music information\n929\tretrieval. The dataset includes user-generated content from\n930\tYouTube, which may reflect inherent demographic, cultural,\n931\tand platform-driven biases. In particular, the distribution\n932\tof languages, genres, and performance styles is influenced\n933\tby upload behavior and search and recommendation mechanisms on the platform. As a result, models trained on\n934\tDiVers may inherit or amplify these biases, and we encourage careful evaluation and reporting of model behavior\n935\tacross different subsets of the data. The dataset is intended\n936\tstrictly for academic research in music information retrieval.\n937\tWhile we do not anticipate direct harmful applications, we\n938\tacknowledge that version identification technologies could,\n939\tin principle, be applied in contexts such as automated copyright enforcement, which may have implications for content\n940\tcreators and rights holders. Researchers should therefore\n941\tconsider the ethical implications of downstream use cases.\n942\tWe release DiVers to support reproducible and large-scale\n943\tresearch in music VI and to encourage the development of\n944\tmore robust and diverse retrieval systems.\n945\t\n946\t[2] G. Doras and G. Peeters, “A prototypical triplet loss for\n947\tcover detection,” in Int. Conf. on Acoustics, Speech and\n948\tSignal Processing. IEEE, 2020, pp. 3797–3801.\n949\t[3] J. Serrà, F. Yesiler, and E. Gómez, “Less is more: faster\n950\tand better music version identification with embedding\n951\tdistillation,” in Proc. of the 21st Int. Society for Music\n952\tInformation Retrieval Conf., 2020, pp. 884–892.\n953\t[4] R. O. Araz, J. Serrà, Y. Mitsufuji, X. Serra, and D. Bogdanov, “Discogs-VINet-MIREX,” in Late-Breaking and\n954\tDemo Session of the 25th Int. Society for Music Information Retrieval Conf., 2024.\n955\t[5] X. Du, Z. Wang, X. Liang, H. Liang, B. Zhu, and Z. Ma,\n956\t“Bytecover3: Accurate cover song identification on short\n957\tqueries,” in Int. Conf. on Acoustics, Speech and Signal\n958\tProcessing. IEEE, 2023, pp. 1–5.\n959\t[6] J. Serrà, R. O. Araz, D. Bogdanov, and Y. Mitsufuji,\n960\t“Supervised contrastive learning from weakly-labeled\n961\taudio segments for musical version matching,” in Proc.\n962\tof the 42nd Int. Conf. on Machine Learning, 2025.\n963\t[7] R. O. Araz, X. Serra, and D. Bogdanov, “Discogs-VI:\n964\tA musical version identification dataset based on public\n965\teditorial metadata,” in Proc. of the 25th Int. Society for\n966\tMusic Information Retrieval Conf., 2024, pp. 478–485.\n967\t[8] J. Affolter, B. Martin, E. V. Epure, G. Meseguer-Brocal,\n968\tand F. Kaplan, “Scalable music cover retrieval using\n969\tlyrics-aligned audio embeddings,” in Proc. of the 48th\n970\tEuropean Conf. on Information Retrieval. Springer,\n971\t2026, pp. 49–66.\n972\t[9] B. McDaniel, “Popular music reaction videos: Reactivity, creator labor, and the performance of listening\n973\tonline,” New Media & Society, vol. 23, no. 6, pp. 1624–\n974\t1641, 2021.\n975\t[10] M. Airoldi, D. Beraldo, and A. Gandini, “Follow the\n976\talgorithm: An exploratory investigation of music on\n977\tyoutube,” Poetics, vol. 57, pp. 1–13, 2016.\n978\t[11] S. Hachmeier and R. Jäschke, “On the robustness of\n979\tcover version identification models: a study using cover\n980\tversions from youtube,” Information Research: an int.\n981\telectronic journal, vol. 30, no. iConf, pp. 1103–1122,\n982\t2025.\n983\t[12] Z. Yu, X. Xu, X. Chen, and D. Yang, “Learning a representation for cover song identification using convolutional neural network,” in Int. Conf. on Acoustics,\n984\tSpeech and Signal Processing. IEEE, 2020, pp. 541–\n985\t545.\n986\t\n987\t\f[13] D. F. Silva, V. M. de Souza, and G. E. Batista, “Music\n988\tshapelets for fast cover song recognition.” in Proc. of\n989\tthe 16th Int. Society for Music Information Retrieval\n990\tConf., 2015, pp. 441–447.\n991\t\n992\t[26] D. Bogdanov, M. Won, P. Tovstogan, A. Porter, and\n993\tX. Serra, “The mtg-jamendo dataset for automatic music\n994\ttagging,” in Machine Learning for Music Discovery\n995\tWorkshop, 36th Int. Conf. on Machine Learning, 2019.\n996\t\n997\t[14] D. P. Ellis and G. E. Poliner, “Identifying cover songs\n998\twith chroma features and dynamic programming beat\n999\ttracking,” in Int. Conf. on Acoustics, Speech and Signal\n1000\tProcessing, vol. 4. IEEE, 2007, pp. IV–1429.\n1001\t\n1002\t[27] E. Law, K. West, M. I. Mandel, M. Bay, and J. S.\n1003\tDownie, “Evaluation of algorithms using games: The\n1004\tcase of music tagging.” in Proc. of the 10th Int. Society for Music Information Retrieval Conf., 2009, pp.\n1005\t387–392.\n1006\t\n1007\t[15] YouTube,\n1008\tLLC,\n1009\t“Fair\n1010\tuse\n1011\ton\n1012\tyoutube,”\n1013\t[Online].\n1014\tAvailable:\n1015\thttps://support.google.com/youtube/answer/9783148,\n1016\t2025, [Accessed: January 15, 2025].\n1017\t[16] H. K. Saini, “youtube-search-python,” [Software] Version 1.6.6. Available: https://pypi.org/project/youtubesearch-python/, 2022, [Accessed: May 7, 2025].\n1018\t[17] F. Yesiler, C. Tralie, A. Correya, D. F. Silva, P. Tovstogan, E. Gómez, and X. Serra, “Da-TACOS: A dataset for\n1019\tcover song identification and understanding,” in Proc.\n1020\tof the 20th Int. Society for Music Information Retrieval\n1021\tConf., 2019, pp. 327–334.\n1022\t[18] S. Hachmeier and R. Jäschke, “A benchmark and robustness study of in-context-learning with large language\n1023\tmodels in music entity detection,” in Proc. of the 31st\n1024\tInt. Conf. on Computational Linguistics, 2025, pp. 9845–\n1025\t9859.\n1026\t[19] M. Bachmann, “Rapidfuzz,” [Software] Version 3.13.0\n1027\tAvailable: https://pypi.org/project/RapidFuzz/, 2025,\n1028\t[Accessed: May 7, 2025].\n1029\t[20] S. Hachmeier and R. Jäschke, “Leveraging usergenerated metadata of online videos for cover song\n1030\tidentification,” in Proc. of the 3rd Workshop on NLP for\n1031\tMusic and Audio, 2024, pp. 43–48.\n1032\t[21] D. Erat, “soundalike,” [Software] Version 0.1.3 Available: https://codeberg.org/derat/soundalike, 2026, [Accessed: March 23, 2026].\n1033\t[22] L. Lalinský, “Chromaprint,” [Software] Version 1.6.0\n1034\tAvailable: https://github.com/acoustid/chromaprint,\n1035\t2026, [Accessed: March 23, 2026].\n1036\t[23] Q. Kong, Y. Cao, T. Iqbal, Y. Wang, W. Wang, and M. D.\n1037\tPlumbley, “Panns: Large-scale pretrained audio neural\n1038\tnetworks for audio pattern recognition,” IEEE/ACM\n1039\tTransactions on Audio, Speech, and Language Processing, vol. 28, pp. 2880–2894, 2020.\n1040\t[24] J. Abeßer, S. Grollmisch, and M. Müller, “How robust\n1041\tare audio embeddings for polyphonic sound event tagging?” IEEE/ACM Transactions on Audio, Speech, and\n1042\tLanguage Processing, vol. 31, pp. 2658–2667, 2023.\n1043\t[25] R. O. Araz, D. Bogdanov, P. Alonso-Jiménez, and\n1044\tF. Font, “Evaluation of deep audio representations for\n1045\tsemantic sound similarity,” in Proc. of the 21st Int. Conf.\n1046\ton Content-Based Multimedia Indexing, 2024, pp. 1–7.\n1047\t\n1048\t[28] A. Singh, A. Fry, A. Perelman, A. Tart, A. Ganesh,\n1049\tA. El-Kishky, A. McLaughlin, A. Low, A. Ostrow,\n1050\tA. Ananthram et al., “Openai gpt-5 system card,” arXiv\n1051\tpreprint arXiv:2601.03267, 2025.\n1052\t[29] S. Hu, B. Zhang, J. Lu, Y. Jiang, W. Wang, L. Kong,\n1053\tW. Zhao, and T. Jiang, “Wideresnet with joint representation learning and data augmentation for cover song\n1054\tidentification.” in Proc. Interspeech, 2022, pp. 4187–\n1055\t4191.\n1056\t[30] D. S. Park, W. Chan, Y. Zhang, C.-C. Chiu, B. Zoph,\n1057\tE. D. Cubuk, and Q. V. Le, “Specaugment: A simple\n1058\tdata augmentation method for automatic speech recognition,” in Proc. Interspeech, 2019, pp. 2613–2617.\n1059\t[31] F. Yesiler, J. Serrà, and E. Gómez, “Accurate and scalable version identification using musically-motivated\n1060\tembeddings,” in Int. Conf. on Acoustics, Speech and\n1061\tSignal Processing. IEEE, 2020, pp. 21–25.\n1062\t[32] X. Du, K. Chen, Z. Wang, B. Zhu, and Z. Ma, “Bytecover2: Towards dimensionality reduction of latent\n1063\tembedding for efficient cover song identification,” in\n1064\tInt. Conf. on Acoustics, Speech and Signal Processing.\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "3c09a112-7bf1-4bbb-ad44-b5fe73cc4ddb", "uuid": "ba1d923e-a408-488a-bc86-57e1a4d28f7d", "timestamp": "2026-08-06T07:13:22.726Z", "tool_use_result": {"type": "text", "file": {"filePath": "/tmp/paper_2608.04543v1.txt", "content": "Model\n\nTrain\n\nDVI\n\n∗\n\nIn-the-Wild\n∗\n\nSHS100K\n\nYVI-S\n\nCombined\n\nYVI-L\n\nDiVers-S\n\nDiVers-L\n\nMAP ↑ NAR ↓ MAP ↑ NAR ↓ MAP ↑ NAR ↓ MAP ↑ NAR ↓ MAP ↑ NAR ↓ MAP ↑ NAR ↓\nByteCover2† [32]\nCLEWS [6]\nDVI\nCLEWSL2 [6]\nCQTNet [12]\nDVINet+ [4]\n\n.562\n.781\n.793\n.488\n.653\n\n5.89\n3.09\n3.03\n6.67\n3.74\n\n.760\n.841\n.849\n.669\n.775\n\n2.75\n1.55\n1.37\n4.28\n2.10\n\n.667\n.775\n.802\n.644\n.720\n\n5.14\n2.54\n1.77\n6.06\n3.40\n\n.707\n.796\n.816\n.647\n.724\n\n4.15\n2.40\n1.91\n5.90\n3.24\n\n.573\n.769\n.785\n.509\n.655\n\n5.64\n2.94\n2.73\n6.49\n3.63\n\n.615\n.778\n.793\n.544\n.670\n\n5.04\n2.78\n2.54\n6.29\n3.49\n\nCLEWSFT\nCLEWSFT+L2\n\nDi-S\n\n.770\n.781\n\n3.24\n3.00\n\n.844\n.857*\n\n1.66\n1.33*\n\n.819\n.845*\n\n1.99\n1.31*\n\n.824\n.842*\n\n1.99\n1.53*\n\n.771\n.787\n\n2.94\n2.61\n\n.784\n.799*\n\n2.65\n2.34*\n\nDVINetX\n\nDVI∗\nDi-S\nDi-L\n\n.669\n.645\n.654\n\n3.67\n3.89\n3.83\n\n.791\n.780\n.796\n\n1.91\n1.89\n1.67\n\n.752\n.790\n.804\n\n2.44\n1.68\n1.45\n\n.756\n.771\n.788\n\n2.47\n1.95\n1.68\n\n.674\n.665\n.676\n\n3.37\n3.34\n3.23\n\n.693\n.687\n.701\n\n3.13\n2.98\n2.82\n\nTable 3. Results of VI systems by training dataset across test subsets. Di abbreviates DiVers and CLEWSFT denotes\nfine-tuned CLEWS. L2 denotes L2 -normalized embeddings at inference time. Checkpoints for trained systems on DVI are\nprovided by the authors of CLEWS [6]. Bold and underlined values indicate the best and second-best results, respectively.\n* indicates a statistically significant improvement of CLEWSFT+L2 over CLEWSL2 (Wilcoxon signed-rank test, HolmBonferroni-corrected p < 0.05).\n\nQuery Candidate\n\n∆ MAP ↑ ∆ NAR ↓\n\nDVI∗\n\ncover & guitar\nkaraoke\nlive\nofficial\ntutorial\ntutorial & guitar\n\n.022*\n.024*\n.018*\n-.002\n.056*\n.080*\n\n-0.20*\n-0.08*\n-0.20*\n-0.42\n-1.14*\n-0.67*\n\nYVI\n\ncover & guitar\nkaraoke\nlive\nofficial\ntutorial\ntutorial & guitar\n\n.028*\n.027*\n.013*\n.005*\n.080*\n.080*\n\n-0.12*\n-0.20*\n-0.20\n-0.36*\n-0.96*\n-0.71*\n\nTable 4. ∆ between CLEWSFT+L2 and CLEWSL2 on stratified subsets across different combinations of query domains\nand candidates stratified by a respective tag. * indicates\nstatistical significance (Wilcoxon signed-rank test, HolmBonferroni-corrected p < 0.05).\n\n3.3.3 Embedding Space Analysis\nWe analyze how fine-tuning affects the embedding space\nof the CLEWS variants on the DVI∗ and YVI test subsets using full-track embeddings computed over 50,000\nrandomly sampled test versions per dataset. We measure\nintra-class distance as the mean pairwise distance between\nembeddings within the same class, and inter-class distance\nanalogously across classes; their ratio (inter/intra) quantifies\nclass separability. Since our best-performing configurations\nuse L2 -normalized embeddings, we focus on that setting\nfirst.\nFine-tuning increases the ratio on YVI from 3.87 to 4.00,\nbut decreases it on DVI∗ from 3.43 to 3.21, mirroring the\n\nMAP degradation reported in Section 3.3.1. Separability on\nin-the-wild versions thus improves at the cost of separability\non cleaner, cataloged ones. The latter is especially consequential in DVI∗ , where a median class size of 3 makes\nMAP sensitive to the rank of individual positives.\nWithout normalization, fine-tuning shrinks both distances, but the intra-class distance shrinks more, so the\nratio still rises on both datasets (e.g., 2.78 → 3.23 on\nDVI∗ ). Normalization then raises the ratio considerably for\nthe baseline (2.78 → 3.43 on DVI∗ ) but only marginally\nfor the fine-tuned model (3.23 → 3.21). A possible explanation is that embedding norms become more uniform\nacross tracks after fine-tuning, with their standard deviation\nroughly halving on both datasets at a nearly constant mean.\nNormalization may therefore alter the mean-distance geometry less after fine-tuning, although it still yields consistent\nretrieval gains (Section 3.3.2).\n\n4. CONCLUSION AND FUTURE WORK\nWe introduce DiVers, a large VI dataset incorporating unofficial and user-generated YouTube recordings, resulting\nin a more acoustically heterogeneous benchmark than existing curated collections. Our experiments suggest that\ntraining and fine-tuning on DiVers can improve robustness\nto noisy and diverse inputs. We further observe that finetuning improves class separability on in-the-wild versions\nwhile reducing it on cleaner ones from DVI∗ , mirroring the\ncorresponding MAP results.\nFuture work should explore methods that better enforce\ninter-class separation and improve the robustness of learned\nrepresentations. Additionally, integrating multi-view signals beyond audio embeddings – such as lyrics [8, 33] –\noffers a promising direction for more robust VI under realworld conditions.\n\n\f5. ACKNOWLEDGMENTS\n\n8. REFERENCES\n\nWe thank our student assistants Sharleen Frankenstein, Siri\nGrenzebach, Jannis Köster and Anna Schwerdtel at the\nBerlin School of Library and Information Science for their\nsupport in annotating the data to evaluate the precision of\nthe automatically matched tags.\nR. Oguz Araz is partially supported by the pre-doctoral\ngrant AGAUR-FI Joan Oró (2024 FI-3 00065) and the Cátedra IA y Música project (TSI-100929-2023-1), funded by\nthe Secretaría de Estado de Digitalización e Inteligencia\nArtificial and European Union-Next Generation EU.\n\n[1] M. Abrassart and G. Doras, “And what if two musical versions don’t share melody, harmony, rhythm, or\nlyrics?” in Proc. of the 23rd Int. Society for Music\nInformation Retrieval Conf., 2022, pp. 677–684.\n\n6. AI USAGE STATEMENT\nWe used large language models (LLMs) in a limited and\nsupportive role during this work. Specifically, LLMs were\nemployed for writing style refinement and language editing,\nas well as for assisting in code generation and subsequent\nrefinement. In addition, LLMs were used to translate the tag\nvocabulary into multiple European languages to improve tag\nmatching coverage. These uses did not constitute a methodological contribution to the research itself. All generated\ncontent, including code and translations, was manually reviewed, validated, and, where necessary, corrected by the\nauthors to ensure accuracy and consistency. The authors\ntake full responsibility for all aspects of the work, including\nthe correctness of the dataset, experiments, and reported\nresults.\n\n7. ETHICS STATEMENT\nThis work introduces DiVers, a collection of large-scale\ndatasets of music versions collected from YouTube. All\naudio content referenced in the dataset is sourced from\npublicly accessible YouTube uploads, and no copyrighted\naudio is redistributed. The release contains only metadata, annotations, and derived feature representations intended for non-commercial research in music information\nretrieval. The dataset includes user-generated content from\nYouTube, which may reflect inherent demographic, cultural,\nand platform-driven biases. In particular, the distribution\nof languages, genres, and performance styles is influenced\nby upload behavior and search and recommendation mechanisms on the platform. As a result, models trained on\nDiVers may inherit or amplify these biases, and we encourage careful evaluation and reporting of model behavior\nacross different subsets of the data. The dataset is intended\nstrictly for academic research in music information retrieval.\nWhile we do not anticipate direct harmful applications, we\nacknowledge that version identification technologies could,\nin principle, be applied in contexts such as automated copyright enforcement, which may have implications for content\ncreators and rights holders. Researchers should therefore\nconsider the ethical implications of downstream use cases.\nWe release DiVers to support reproducible and large-scale\nresearch in music VI and to encourage the development of\nmore robust and diverse retrieval systems.\n\n[2] G. Doras and G. Peeters, “A prototypical triplet loss for\ncover detection,” in Int. Conf. on Acoustics, Speech and\nSignal Processing. IEEE, 2020, pp. 3797–3801.\n[3] J. Serrà, F. Yesiler, and E. Gómez, “Less is more: faster\nand better music version identification with embedding\ndistillation,” in Proc. of the 21st Int. Society for Music\nInformation Retrieval Conf., 2020, pp. 884–892.\n[4] R. O. Araz, J. Serrà, Y. Mitsufuji, X. Serra, and D. Bogdanov, “Discogs-VINet-MIREX,” in Late-Breaking and\nDemo Session of the 25th Int. Society for Music Information Retrieval Conf., 2024.\n[5] X. Du, Z. Wang, X. Liang, H. Liang, B. Zhu, and Z. Ma,\n“Bytecover3: Accurate cover song identification on short\nqueries,” in Int. Conf. on Acoustics, Speech and Signal\nProcessing. IEEE, 2023, pp. 1–5.\n[6] J. Serrà, R. O. Araz, D. Bogdanov, and Y. Mitsufuji,\n“Supervised contrastive learning from weakly-labeled\naudio segments for musical version matching,” in Proc.\nof the 42nd Int. Conf. on Machine Learning, 2025.\n[7] R. O. Araz, X. Serra, and D. Bogdanov, “Discogs-VI:\nA musical version identification dataset based on public\neditorial metadata,” in Proc. of the 25th Int. Society for\nMusic Information Retrieval Conf., 2024, pp. 478–485.\n[8] J. Affolter, B. Martin, E. V. Epure, G. Meseguer-Brocal,\nand F. Kaplan, “Scalable music cover retrieval using\nlyrics-aligned audio embeddings,” in Proc. of the 48th\nEuropean Conf. on Information Retrieval. Springer,\n2026, pp. 49–66.\n[9] B. McDaniel, “Popular music reaction videos: Reactivity, creator labor, and the performance of listening\nonline,” New Media & Society, vol. 23, no. 6, pp. 1624–\n1641, 2021.\n[10] M. Airoldi, D. Beraldo, and A. Gandini, “Follow the\nalgorithm: An exploratory investigation of music on\nyoutube,” Poetics, vol. 57, pp. 1–13, 2016.\n[11] S. Hachmeier and R. Jäschke, “On the robustness of\ncover version identification models: a study using cover\nversions from youtube,” Information Research: an int.\nelectronic journal, vol. 30, no. iConf, pp. 1103–1122,\n2025.\n[12] Z. Yu, X. Xu, X. Chen, and D. Yang, “Learning a representation for cover song identification using convolutional neural network,” in Int. Conf. on Acoustics,\nSpeech and Signal Processing. IEEE, 2020, pp. 541–\n545.\n\n\f[13] D. F. Silva, V. M. de Souza, and G. E. Batista, “Music\nshapelets for fast cover song recognition.” in Proc. of\nthe 16th Int. Society for Music Information Retrieval\nConf., 2015, pp. 441–447.\n\n[26] D. Bogdanov, M. Won, P. Tovstogan, A. Porter, and\nX. Serra, “The mtg-jamendo dataset for automatic music\ntagging,” in Machine Learning for Music Discovery\nWorkshop, 36th Int. Conf. on Machine Learning, 2019.\n\n[14] D. P. Ellis and G. E. Poliner, “Identifying cover songs\nwith chroma features and dynamic programming beat\ntracking,” in Int. Conf. on Acoustics, Speech and Signal\nProcessing, vol. 4. IEEE, 2007, pp. IV–1429.\n\n[27] E. Law, K. West, M. I. Mandel, M. Bay, and J. S.\nDownie, “Evaluation of algorithms using games: The\ncase of music tagging.” in Proc. of the 10th Int. Society for Music Information Retrieval Conf., 2009, pp.\n387–392.\n\n[15] YouTube,\nLLC,\n“Fair\nuse\non\nyoutube,”\n[Online].\nAvailable:\nhttps://support.google.com/youtube/answer/9783148,\n2025, [Accessed: January 15, 2025].\n[16] H. K. Saini, “youtube-search-python,” [Software] Version 1.6.6. Available: https://pypi.org/project/youtubesearch-python/, 2022, [Accessed: May 7, 2025].\n[17] F. Yesiler, C. Tralie, A. Correya, D. F. Silva, P. Tovstogan, E. Gómez, and X. Serra, “Da-TACOS: A dataset for\ncover song identification and understanding,” in Proc.\nof the 20th Int. Society for Music Information Retrieval\nConf., 2019, pp. 327–334.\n[18] S. Hachmeier and R. Jäschke, “A benchmark and robustness study of in-context-learning with large language\nmodels in music entity detection,” in Proc. of the 31st\nInt. Conf. on Computational Linguistics, 2025, pp. 9845–\n9859.\n[19] M. Bachmann, “Rapidfuzz,” [Software] Version 3.13.0\nAvailable: https://pypi.org/project/RapidFuzz/, 2025,\n[Accessed: May 7, 2025].\n[20] S. Hachmeier and R. Jäschke, “Leveraging usergenerated metadata of online videos for cover song\nidentification,” in Proc. of the 3rd Workshop on NLP for\nMusic and Audio, 2024, pp. 43–48.\n[21] D. Erat, “soundalike,” [Software] Version 0.1.3 Available: https://codeberg.org/derat/soundalike, 2026, [Accessed: March 23, 2026].\n[22] L. Lalinský, “Chromaprint,” [Software] Version 1.6.0\nAvailable: https://github.com/acoustid/chromaprint,\n2026, [Accessed: March 23, 2026].\n[23] Q. Kong, Y. Cao, T. Iqbal, Y. Wang, W. Wang, and M. D.\nPlumbley, “Panns: Large-scale pretrained audio neural\nnetworks for audio pattern recognition,” IEEE/ACM\nTransactions on Audio, Speech, and Language Processing, vol. 28, pp. 2880–2894, 2020.\n[24] J. Abeßer, S. Grollmisch, and M. Müller, “How robust\nare audio embeddings for polyphonic sound event tagging?” IEEE/ACM Transactions on Audio, Speech, and\nLanguage Processing, vol. 31, pp. 2658–2667, 2023.\n[25] R. O. Araz, D. Bogdanov, P. Alonso-Jiménez, and\nF. Font, “Evaluation of deep audio representations for\nsemantic sound similarity,” in Proc. of the 21st Int. Conf.\non Content-Based Multimedia Indexing, 2024, pp. 1–7.\n\n[28] A. Singh, A. Fry, A. Perelman, A. Tart, A. Ganesh,\nA. El-Kishky, A. McLaughlin, A. Low, A. Ostrow,\nA. Ananthram et al., “Openai gpt-5 system card,” arXiv\npreprint arXiv:2601.03267, 2025.\n[29] S. Hu, B. Zhang, J. Lu, Y. Jiang, W. Wang, L. Kong,\nW. Zhao, and T. Jiang, “Wideresnet with joint representation learning and data augmentation for cover song\nidentification.” in Proc. Interspeech, 2022, pp. 4187–\n4191.\n[30] D. S. Park, W. Chan, Y. Zhang, C.-C. Chiu, B. Zoph,\nE. D. Cubuk, and Q. V. Le, “Specaugment: A simple\ndata augmentation method for automatic speech recognition,” in Proc. Interspeech, 2019, pp. 2613–2617.\n[31] F. Yesiler, J. Serrà, and E. Gómez, “Accurate and scalable version identification using musically-motivated\nembeddings,” in Int. Conf. on Acoustics, Speech and\nSignal Processing. IEEE, 2020, pp. 21–25.\n[32] X. Du, K. Chen, Z. Wang, B. Zhu, and Z. Ma, “Bytecover2: Towards dimensionality reduction of latent\nembedding for efficient cover song identification,” in\nInt. Conf. on Acoustics, Speech and Signal Processing.", "numLines": 470, "startLine": 595, "totalLines": 1071}}}
